        <ArticleTitle>Machine learning approaches for systematic literature reviews in biomedical research: a comprehensive analysis</ArticleTitle>
        <Abstract>
          <AbstractText>Systematic literature reviews (SLRs) are essential for synthesizing evidence in biomedical research. However, the manual screening process is time-consuming and prone to human error. This study evaluates machine learning (ML) approaches to automate title and abstract screening in SLRs. We analyzed 42 studies published between 2015 and 2023, comparing performance metrics of supervised, semi-supervised, and active learning methods. Results indicate that active learning combined with transformer-based models achieved the highest recall (0.97) with a workload reduction of 62%. Our findings support the integration of ML tools into systematic review workflows to improve efficiency without compromising completeness.</AbstractText>
        </Abstract>
