        <ArticleTitle>Inter-rater reliability in systematic review screening: impact of training, topic familiarity, and dual vs single reviewer strategies</ArticleTitle>
        <Abstract>
          <AbstractText>Inter-rater reliability (IRR) during title/abstract screening significantly impacts systematic review quality. We investigated factors influencing IRR across 28 research teams conducting systematic reviews in clinical medicine. Teams using structured training protocols achieved kappa values ≥ 0.70 in 89% of cases, compared to 54% in untrained teams. Topic familiarity was positively associated with IRR (r = 0.61, p &lt; 0.001). Dual-reviewer screening strategies consistently outperformed single-reviewer approaches in sensitivity (0.98 vs 0.87). We recommend mandatory calibration exercises before screening and periodic recalibration during large-scale reviews to maintain acceptable IRR throughout the process.</AbstractText>
        </Abstract>
